# What: Manifest Content Type Rule. Why: Netlify serves .webmanifest files as application/octet-stream, which Chrome tolerates but Firefox and some install paths reject. How: This forces the manifest's correct type.
/manifest.webmanifest
	Content-Type: application/manifest+json



# _headers = Security Headers Policy
#
# @summary
# Hardening headers applied to every response. The app is entirely client
# only, with no backend, API, or accounts, and loads nothing from a third
# party: every script, style, font, and icon is self-hosted, and its only
# network write is the support form's Netlify Forms POST
# (tabs/settings/contact-support.jsx), which goes to this same origin. That
# lets the Content-Security-Policy be genuinely strict rather than needing
# broad allowances.
#
# default-src 'self' is the fallback every unlisted directive inherits, so
# connect-src, font-src, img-src, manifest-src, and worker-src need no lines
# of their own. The directives listed either need a different value than
# 'self' (object-src, frame-src, style-src), are never covered by
# default-src at all (frame-ancestors, form-action), or just state the
# default for clarity (base-uri, script-src).
#
# style-src needs 'unsafe-inline' for two reasons: the boot splash's
# critical CSS in index.html is inline on purpose, since an external
# stylesheet would add a network round trip before it can paint, and React
# renders many components' dynamic sizing and positioning through inline
# style attributes (e.g. src/ui/bg-flourish.jsx). Both fall under style-src,
# not script-src, so this doesn't weaken protection against inline scripts.
# The boot splash's dismiss script is its own file (public/boot-splash.js)
# precisely so script-src needs no such allowance.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security keeps browsers on HTTPS for two years across
# every subdomain (www already redirects over HTTPS through Cloudflare),
# replacing Netlify's default of one year for this host only. It leaves out
# preload, which commits the domain to the browsers' built-in HTTPS list and
# is slow to undo. Referrer-Policy sends a referrer only to this same origin,
# so the outbound links (the legal generators, the support site, GitHub)
# never learn which page a visitor came from. Permissions-Policy turns off
# every powerful browser feature the app never uses, leaving the ones it does
# (the clipboard, storage, the service worker, and notifications, which no
# policy covers) untouched.
#
# @author z4nta0 <https://github.com/z4nta0>

# What: Security Headers Rule. Why: Every response should refuse framing, sniffing, plain HTTP, unused browser features, and anything loaded from another origin. How: This applies the Content-Security-Policy, the permissions and referrer policies, HSTS, a nosniff content type, and a deny on framing to every path.
/*
	Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; frame-src 'none'; object-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'
	Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), browsing-topics=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), hid=(), idle-detection=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), picture-in-picture=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), serial=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
	Referrer-Policy: same-origin
	Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
	X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
	X-Frame-Options: DENY
